#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cw-theme-sync -- make Claude Code's TUI theme follow the browser theme.

WHY THIS EXISTS (the "Reasons" a naive approach fails)
------------------------------------------------------
The ttyd browser terminal already flips xterm.js's *terminal colours*
(background / ANSI palette) to match `prefers-color-scheme` -- see
examples/compose/ttyd/inject-autodark.py. Claude Code's `theme` setting,
when left at "auto", picks light vs dark by probing the terminal's
background colour AT PROCESS STARTUP. So a browser started in dark mode
statically gets a dark Claude TUI. But "auto" is resolved ONCE, at
startup: Claude Code does NOT re-probe the terminal background when it
changes mid-session, so flipping the browser/OS colour scheme while a
session is live leaves the TUI theme stuck. That is the "it doesn't work
dynamically" wall.

THE MECHANISM
-------------
Explicit re-set instead of relying on startup auto-detect:

  browser (prefers-color-scheme change)
    -> POST /theme-report  (clipboard-upload sidecar, examples/compose)
    -> sidecar atomically writes "dark"/"light" to CW_THEME_FILE
       (default /host-clipboard/theme, on the shared volume)
    -> THIS daemon (running inside claude-container) notices the file
       change and injects `/config theme=<dark|light>` into the Claude
       Code tmux pane.

`/config` is a NON-INTERACTIVE slash command (Claude Code >= ~2.1;
`supportsNonInteractive`, `argumentHint:"key=value"`): `/config
theme=dark` sets the theme by key and hot-applies + re-renders WITHOUT a
restart, so the swap is live. (Side effect: it persists an explicit
`theme` value into the settings file, overriding "auto" -- intended: we
are taking over theme control while a browser drives it.)

DESIGN NOTES
------------
  * Idle-gated AND non-cancelling. We only inject when the pane shows an
    idle prompt (the cursor glyph), AND we inject via a plain
    `claude-watch inject` -- which since 2026-08-18 NEVER leads with an
    Escape unless `--escape` is passed. The user's browser theme toggle
    must not cancel the model's in-flight turn, so this script must never
    grow that flag. The idle gate is best-effort (there is a race between
    the check and the inject); the non-cancelling inject is the hard
    guarantee: even if the pane went busy in that window, the `/config
    theme=…` slash command is typed + submitted as a QUEUED message (bare
    Enter from INSERT, no Escape/`dd`), so it lands at the next turn
    boundary instead of interrupting. If the pane is busy at check time the
    desired theme is remembered and applied on a later poll.
  * Debounced / coalescing. We always inject the CURRENT file value, so a
    rapid light->dark->light flip only results in the final theme.
  * Idempotent. We track the last theme we injected and skip no-op
    re-injects.
  * Serialised with EVERY other injector -- inside `claude-watch inject`.
    All injectors drive `tmux send-keys` on the same prompt line, and the
    non-cancelling choreography has no `dd` line-clear, so concurrent
    payloads INTERLEAVE. Observed 2026-08-19: this daemon's
    `/config theme=light` landed spliced into the middle of the word
    "6min" in cw-watcher-health-check's WATCHER DOWN banner --

        unconsumed >6mi/config theme=lightn -- the event watcher is dead

    -- so Claude Code never saw a slash command, the theme never changed,
    and this daemon logged "(verified)" anyway. The lock now lives INSIDE
    `claude-watch inject` (src/inject_lock.rs) rather than in each caller,
    because the daemon's own in-process alerts never shell out to
    `claude-watch inject` at all -- no caller-side convention could cover
    both racing populations. We still take the legacy self-clear flock
    below, but that is only a "stay off the pane during a self-clear"
    courtesy, not the interleave guard.

  * Verified against the EFFECT, not the prompt line. `claude-watch
    inject` reports success when the payload CLEARS from the input line --
    equally true when MANGLED text gets submitted, which is exactly how
    the failure above logged "(verified)". After a successful inject we
    read `theme` back out of Claude Code's settings file (`/config`
    persists an explicit value there) and confirm it matches.

  * Re-assertable on demand. A same-value write to the theme file is a
    no-op by design, so a TUI that drifted out of sync could not be
    corrected without toggling the browser theme twice. `--force` injects
    the current file value regardless of what we think is applied, and
    SIGHUP makes the running daemon re-assert on its next poll.
  * The idle gate reads COLOUR, not just glyphs. Claude Code renders a
    suggestion inside an EMPTY input box as DIM (SGR 2) ghost text. Because
    the pane was captured WITHOUT `-e`, that attribute was stripped and the
    ghost was indistinguishable from typed input, so the "input line must be
    empty" gate latched shut for as long as a suggestion was on screen --
    hours, and silently. `_capture` now passes `-e` and `_prompt_line`
    discards dim runs. Do not drop `-e` to "simplify" the capture.

  * A shut gate is LOUD. Waiting for idle used to produce no output
    whatsoever, so "blocked for three hours" and "daemon dead" looked
    identical in the log. The daemon now logs which gate is holding, and for
    how long, every CW_THEME_BLOCKED_LOG_SECS.

  * Dependency-free. Pure mtime poll (no inotify), tiny cost; theme
    changes are rare.

EXTERNAL HOOKS (theme-hooks.d)
------------------------------
The daemon has exactly ONE built-in job: the `/config theme=` inject. Any
OTHER thing a deployment wants to happen on a theme change -- poke a kiosk,
flip a host app, emit an event -- goes in an executable drop-in under
CW_THEME_HOOKS_DIR (default /etc/claude-watch/theme-hooks.d) instead of a
forked copy of this script.

Two events, both fired from the same flat dir:

  changed   the theme file's VALIDATED VALUE changed (dark<->light),
            immediately, BEFORE the idle gate. This split is load-bearing:
            the idle gate can legitimately stay shut for HOURS (that is what
            BLOCKED_LOG_SECS exists to make visible), and an external side
            effect must not be hostage to whether the operator's prompt line
            happens to be empty. Does NOT fire on an unreadable file, an
            unrecognised value, or a same-value rewrite.
  applied   after inject_theme() returns True -- i.e. settings.json has been
            read back and VERIFIED. Does NOT fire on a failed/unverified
            inject, on the GIVING UP path, or on a --force that timed out
            waiting for idle. Use this when a hook must track the ACTUAL TUI
            state rather than the reported one.

Invocation contract:

    exec <hook> <event> <new_theme>

argv stays at TWO positional args forever -- a positional list is a
compatibility trap the moment you want a third field -- so everything else
is env, overlaid on the daemon's own environment:

    CW_THEME_EVENT          changed | applied
    CW_THEME_NEW            dark | light (same as $2)
    CW_THEME_OLD            previous value, empty on the first observation.
                            For `changed` that is the previous FILE value;
                            for `applied`, the previously APPLIED value.
    CW_THEME_REASON         startup | change | force | sighup
    CW_THEME_SOURCE_FILE    resolved CW_THEME_FILE
    CW_THEME_TIMESTAMP      unix epoch (int)
    CW_THEME_TIMESTAMP_ISO  local ISO-8601, matching the log format
    CW_THEME_PANE           resolved pane -- `applied` ONLY. It is REMOVED
                            from the hook environment on `changed`, even
                            when the daemon itself was configured with it,
                            so a hook can test for its presence.
    CW_THEME_HOOK           the hook's own filename

cwd=/, stdin=/dev/null, stdout+stderr merged and logged (truncated).

FORWARD-COMPATIBILITY RULE, part of the contract: **a hook MUST exit 0 on
an event name it does not recognise.** That is what makes adding a third
event later (the obvious candidate: `failed`, on the GIVING UP path, for
alerting) a non-breaking change. Guard with a `case`, do not assume.

Discovery + execution:
  * run-parts conventions: direct children only, executable regular files
    (or symlinks to them -- `chmod -x` is the disable switch), names
    matching ^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$ so `10-kiosk.sh.bak`, `.disabled` and editor
    backups can never silently fire. NN-name numeric prefixes by convention.
  * Sequential, C-locale BYTE order. Not parallel: theme changes are rare,
    ordering is occasionally meaningful, and a thread pool buys nothing.
  * Re-discovered on EVERY fire, so adding a hook needs no restart.
  * Per-hook timeout (CW_THEME_HOOK_TIMEOUT_SECS, default 10s). On timeout:
    SIGKILL, log, continue.
  * Failure isolation is ABSOLUTE. Non-zero exit, timeout, OSError, decode
    error -- all logged and swallowed; run_hooks() never raises. A hook can
    never fail the inject path. Same default-open posture as tools/hooks/.
  * Worst case is N_hooks x timeout of daemon stall. A hook that needs to do
    long work SELF-BACKGROUNDS -- and must redirect the background child's
    stdout/stderr (`cmd >/dev/null 2>&1 &`), or the inherited pipe keeps the
    hook "running" until the timeout kills it. Theme changes during a stall
    are still coalesced (we always read the CURRENT file value), so
    correctness holds.
  * Each hook runs in its OWN PROCESS GROUP, so a timeout kills the whole
    tree rather than the shell that happened to be PID 1 of the hook.
  * OFF BY DEFAULT, BY ABSENCE. No dir => empty list => byte-identical
    behaviour to a build without this feature, and not one line of log.
  * ANTI-PATTERN: a hook must never write CW_THEME_FILE. Coalescing makes
    such a loop self-limiting, but don't.
  * SECURITY: hooks run with the daemon's identity, inside the blast radius
    of the Claude Code session. The dir wants to be root-owned 0755 with
    0755 root:root files -- a user-writable hooks dir is a privilege path.

Configuration (env, all optional):
  CW_THEME_FILE            theme report file to watch
                           (default /host-clipboard/theme)
  CW_THEME_PANE            tmux pane target (default: auto-resolve via
                           `claude-watch status --json`, else
                           claude-container:0.0)
  CW_THEME_POLL_SECS       mtime poll interval (default 1.5)
  CW_THEME_IDLE_POLL_SECS  how often to re-check idle while a change is
                           pending (default 2.0)
  CW_THEME_LOG             log path (default
                           $XDG_STATE_HOME/claude-watch/theme-sync.log
                           or /var/log/claude-watch/theme-sync.log)
  CW_THEME_LOCK            legacy self-clear coordination lock (default
                           matches self-clear:
                           $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/claude-self-clear.lock or
                           /var/run/claude/claude-self-clear.lock). NOT the
                           inject interleave guard -- that lives inside
                           `claude-watch inject`.
  CW_THEME_SETTINGS_FILE   Claude Code settings file to read `theme` back
                           from for verification (default
                           $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/settings.json, else
                           ~/.claude/settings.json)
  CW_THEME_VERIFY_SECS     how long to wait for settings.json to show the
                           requested theme (default 10.0)
  CW_THEME_BLOCKED_LOG_SECS
                           how often to log which gate is blocking a pending
                           theme change (default 300)
  CW_THEME_HOOKS_DIR       directory of executable theme-change hooks
                           (default /etc/claude-watch/theme-hooks.d). A
                           missing dir means no hooks and no output --
                           that is how the feature stays off by default.
                           Container shapes point this at a mounted path.
  CW_THEME_HOOK_TIMEOUT_SECS
                           per-hook wall-clock timeout before SIGKILL
                           (default 10.0)

Flags:
  --status   print resolved config, current file value, whether the TUI is
             in sync, and the hooks dir + the hooks discovered in it (so a
             typo'd path or a hook that lost its +x is visible without
             reading the log); exit 0
  --force    inject the CURRENT theme-file value once, regardless of
             whether it changed, then exit. Use to re-assert a TUI that
             has drifted out of sync with the file.

Signals:
  SIGHUP     running daemon forgets what it thinks is applied and
             re-asserts the current theme file value on the next poll.

Runs as a long-lived process under process-compose (see
container/process-compose.yml). Disable with
CLAUDE_CONTAINER_THEME_SYNC_DISABLED=true.
"""

import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from datetime import datetime
from pathlib import Path

# Browser reports one of these; map straight onto Claude Code theme keys.
# We keep it to the two base themes the browser's prefers-color-scheme can
# express; a user who wants the daltonized / ansi variants sets `theme`
# explicitly and would not run this daemon.
VALID = ("dark", "light")

_HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")


def _env(name, default):
    v = os.environ.get(name, "").strip()
    return v if v else default


def _default_log_file():
    state_home = os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME", "").strip()
    if state_home:
        return str(Path(state_home) / "claude-watch" / "theme-sync.log")
    return "/var/log/claude-watch/theme-sync.log"


def _default_lock_file():
    runtime = os.environ.get("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR", "").strip()
    if runtime:
        return str(Path(runtime) / "claude-self-clear.lock")
    return "/var/run/claude/claude-self-clear.lock"


def _default_settings_file():
    """Claude Code's settings file -- where `/config theme=X` persists the
    value we then read back to VERIFY the inject actually took effect."""
    cfg_dir = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR", "").strip()
    if cfg_dir:
        return str(Path(cfg_dir) / "settings.json")
    return str(Path.home() / ".claude" / "settings.json")


THEME_FILE = _env("CW_THEME_FILE", "/host-clipboard/theme")
POLL_SECS = float(_env("CW_THEME_POLL_SECS", "1.5"))
IDLE_POLL_SECS = float(_env("CW_THEME_IDLE_POLL_SECS", "2.0"))
LOG_FILE = _env("CW_THEME_LOG", _default_log_file())
LOCK_FILE = _env("CW_THEME_LOCK", _default_lock_file())
SETTINGS_FILE = _env("CW_THEME_SETTINGS_FILE", _default_settings_file())
VERIFY_SECS = float(_env("CW_THEME_VERIFY_SECS", "10.0"))

# Drop-in dir for external theme hooks. Deliberately an /etc path: hooks are
# root-installed drop-ins, and picking the canonical location up front means
# a host deployment needs no unit change to use them. OFF BY DEFAULT BY
# ABSENCE -- no dir, no hooks, no log lines.
HOOKS_DIR = _env("CW_THEME_HOOKS_DIR", "/etc/claude-watch/theme-hooks.d")
HOOK_TIMEOUT_SECS = float(_env("CW_THEME_HOOK_TIMEOUT_SECS", "10.0"))

# After this many consecutive verified-failures for the SAME theme value we
# stop retrying it. Without a cap, a genuinely broken /config (Claude Code
# too old, settings file not writable, pane not actually Claude) would have
# us re-injecting into the operator's prompt line every poll, forever.
MAX_VERIFY_FAILURES = 3

# Delay before retry attempt N (seconds). An unbounded retry against a SHARED
# prompt line is a self-inflicted denial of service: on 2026-08-19 the
# every-poll retry put three mangled payloads into the live pane in ~90s,
# which is worse than the silent failure it replaced. Back off hard, then
# give up -- a wrong-coloured TUI is much cheaper than a corrupted input
# stream.
RETRY_BACKOFF_SECS = (30.0, 120.0, 300.0)

# How long the input line must stay empty before we believe the pane is
# genuinely idle rather than being typed into right now.
PROMPT_RECHECK_SECS = float(_env("CW_THEME_PROMPT_RECHECK_SECS", "0.8"))

# How often to log "still waiting for an idle pane" while a theme change is
# stuck behind the idle gate. The gate can legitimately stay shut for a long
# time on a busy main loop, but it must never do so SILENTLY -- silence is
# how a wedged gate passed for a healthy daemon until 2026-09-01.
BLOCKED_LOG_SECS = float(_env("CW_THEME_BLOCKED_LOG_SECS", "300"))

# Set by SIGHUP: forget what we think is applied and re-assert.
_reassert_requested = False

# How long `--force` waits for a busy pane to go idle before giving up. It
# gives up rather than injecting into a busy pane, because forcing the issue
# would mean --escape, and --escape cancels the in-flight turn (Andrew,
# 2026-08-19: "i do NOT want you to inject escape bc that interrupts").
FORCE_IDLE_WAIT_SECS = float(_env("CW_THEME_FORCE_IDLE_WAIT_SECS", "60.0"))


def _on_sighup(_signum, _frame):
    """SIGHUP => re-assert the current theme on the next poll.

    Gives a running daemon the same escape hatch `--force` gives a one-shot
    caller, without a second process racing it onto the pane.
    """
    global _reassert_requested
    _reassert_requested = True


def log(msg):
    line = f"[{datetime.now().strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S')}] {msg}"
    try:
        with open(LOG_FILE, "a") as f:
            f.write(line + "\n")
    except Exception:
        pass
    try:
        print(line, file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
    except Exception:
        pass


def run(cmd, timeout=10):
    """Run a subprocess; return (stdout, rc). Never raises."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
        return r.stdout.strip(), r.returncode
    except Exception:
        return "", -1


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# External hooks (theme-hooks.d) -- see EXTERNAL HOOKS in the module docstring
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# run-parts' name filter, and it is the load-bearing half of discovery: it is
# what stops `10-kiosk.sh.bak`, `20-notify.disabled` and `30-x~` from firing
# behind an operator's back. A dot or a tilde anywhere disqualifies the file.
_HOOK_NAME_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$")

# How much of a hook's merged stdout+stderr reaches the log. Enough to see
# `curl: (7) connection refused`; not enough for a chatty hook to bury the
# daemon's own lines.
HOOK_OUTPUT_LINES = 5
HOOK_OUTPUT_CHARS = 500


def discover_hooks():
    """Executable drop-ins in HOOKS_DIR, in C-locale byte order. Never raises.

    run-parts conventions, because those are the ones every sysadmin already
    has the reflexes for: direct children only (a subdir is skipped, not
    recursed), regular files or symlinks to them, executable bit required
    (`chmod -x` is the disable switch), names restricted by _HOOK_NAME_RE.

    A missing / unreadable dir returns [] rather than raising: absence of the
    dir is how the whole feature stays off, so it is a normal state, not an
    error. Called on EVERY fire so a newly-installed hook needs no restart.
    """
    try:
        entries = os.listdir(HOOKS_DIR)
    except OSError:
        return []
    hooks = []
    # Sort on the encoded name, not the str: the contract says BYTE order, so
    # `10-a` sorts before `2-c`. Naming is ASCII-only per _HOOK_NAME_RE, so
    # this is belt-and-braces, but the contract is what is documented.
    for name in sorted(entries, key=lambda n: n.encode("utf-8", "surrogateescape")):
        if not _HOOK_NAME_RE.match(name):
            continue
        path = Path(HOOKS_DIR) / name
        try:
            if not path.is_file():
                continue
            if not os.access(str(path), os.X_OK):
                continue
        except OSError:
            continue
        hooks.append(path)
    return hooks


def _hook_env(event, new, old, reason, pane, hook_name):
    """The daemon's environment overlaid with the CW_THEME_* contract."""
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env.update({
        "CW_THEME_EVENT": event,
        "CW_THEME_NEW": new,
        "CW_THEME_OLD": old or "",
        "CW_THEME_REASON": reason,
        "CW_THEME_SOURCE_FILE": THEME_FILE,
        "CW_THEME_TIMESTAMP": str(int(time.time())),
        "CW_THEME_TIMESTAMP_ISO": datetime.now().strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S"),
        "CW_THEME_HOOK": hook_name,
    })
    if pane:
        env["CW_THEME_PANE"] = pane
    else:
        # `applied`-only, and that has to be true of the ABSENCE too. The
        # daemon may itself have been configured with CW_THEME_PANE (it is a
        # documented knob), and inheriting it on a `changed` fire would hand
        # a hook a pane it must not assume is meaningful for that event.
        env.pop("CW_THEME_PANE", None)
    return env


def _hook_output_tail(text):
    """First few lines of a hook's merged output, marked when truncated."""
    if not text:
        return ""
    lines = [ln.strip() for ln in text.strip().splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    if not lines:
        return ""
    truncated = len(lines) > HOOK_OUTPUT_LINES
    joined = " | ".join(lines[:HOOK_OUTPUT_LINES])
    if len(joined) > HOOK_OUTPUT_CHARS:
        joined = joined[:HOOK_OUTPUT_CHARS]
        truncated = True
    return f" -- {joined}" + (" [truncated]" if truncated else "")


def _run_one_hook(hook, event, new, old, reason, pane):
    """Run ONE hook to completion (or to its timeout). Never raises."""
    name = hook.name
    tag = f"hook {name} ({event} {new})"
    started = time.monotonic()
    try:
        devnull = open(os.devnull, "rb")
    except OSError as e:
        log(f"{tag}: FAILED to open /dev/null -- {e}")
        return
    try:
        try:
            proc = subprocess.Popen(
                [str(hook), event, new],
                stdin=devnull,
                stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
                cwd="/",
                env=_hook_env(event, new, old, reason, pane, name),
                text=True,
                errors="replace",
                # Own process group, so the timeout can kill the whole TREE.
                # Killing just the shell leaves its `sleep`/`curl` child alive
                # AND still holding the stdout pipe, which turns a bounded
                # 10s timeout into an unbounded wait on communicate().
                start_new_session=True,
            )
        except OSError as e:
            # Lost its +x between discovery and exec, bad interpreter line,
            # dangling symlink. Not our problem to fix, only to report.
            log(f"{tag}: FAILED to start -- {e}")
            return
        try:
            out, _ = proc.communicate(timeout=HOOK_TIMEOUT_SECS)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            try:
                os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
            except OSError:
                proc.kill()
            try:
                out, _ = proc.communicate(timeout=5)
            except Exception:
                out = ""
            log(
                f"{tag}: TIMEOUT after {HOOK_TIMEOUT_SECS:.1f}s; killed"
                f"{_hook_output_tail(out)}"
            )
            return
        elapsed = time.monotonic() - started
        if proc.returncode == 0:
            log(f"{tag}: ok in {elapsed:.2f}s{_hook_output_tail(out)}")
        else:
            log(
                f"{tag}: FAILED rc={proc.returncode} in {elapsed:.2f}s"
                f"{_hook_output_tail(out)}"
            )
    finally:
        try:
            devnull.close()
        except Exception:
            pass


def run_hooks(event, new, old=None, reason="change", pane=None):
    """Fire every discovered hook for `event`, sequentially. NEVER RAISES.

    This is the one hard property of the whole feature: an external hook must
    not be able to break the inject path, so every failure mode -- non-zero
    exit, timeout, exec error, a bug in this runner -- is logged and
    swallowed. Same default-open posture as tools/hooks/.

    Silent when the hooks dir does not exist, which is the default state.
    """
    try:
        hooks = discover_hooks()
        if not hooks:
            return
        log(f"firing {len(hooks)} {event} hook(s) from {HOOKS_DIR}")
        for hook in hooks:
            # Per-hook, not per-batch: a bug in the runner itself must not
            # cost the REMAINING hooks their turn either.
            try:
                _run_one_hook(hook, event, new, old, reason, pane)
            except Exception as e:  # pragma: no cover - defence in depth
                log(f"hook {hook.name} ({event} {new}): runner error {e!r}")
    except Exception as e:  # pragma: no cover - the last-resort net
        log(f"hook runner error on {event} {new}: {e!r} (ignored)")


def resolve_pane():
    """Resolve the Claude Code tmux pane.

    Command-name INDEPENDENT: the native installer runs claude as a
    version-string command, so we never match on command name. Prefer
    `claude-watch status --json` (authoritative), then the env override,
    then the conventional container pane. `claude-watch inject` does its
    own resolution too, but we need a concrete pane for the idle check.
    """
    env_pane = os.environ.get("CW_THEME_PANE", "").strip()
    if env_pane:
        return env_pane
    try:
        out, _ = run(["claude-watch", "status", "--json"])
        data = json.loads(out)
        pane = data.get("pane") or data.get("pane_id")
        if pane:
            return pane
    except Exception:
        pass
    return "claude-container:0.0"


# Glyph class is a RANGE, not a hand-listed set. Claude Code rotates the
# indicator glyph, and a hand-listed set FAILS OPEN on the one you forgot:
# U+273D was missing from the first draft, so a live thinking pane read IDLE.
_BUSY_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*[\u2722-\u274b\u00b7\u25cb-\u25d3*+]\s+\S.*\u2026\s*\(", re.M)


def _capture(pane):
    """Capture the pane with wrapped lines JOINED (`-J`) and ATTRIBUTES KEPT
    (`-e`).

    `-J` is load-bearing. Without it tmux truncates each visual line at the
    pane width, and the status bar renders as
        "-- INSERT -- ⏵⏵ bypass permissions on · 3 shells · ← for agen…"
    with "esc to interrupt" CUT OFF the end. A busy-check keyed on that string
    then reports idle for a pane with a turn in flight — which is exactly how
    a theme inject got queued behind a live turn on 2026-08-19.

    `-e` is load-bearing too, for the opposite failure. Claude Code renders a
    SUGGESTION inside an otherwise-EMPTY input box as DIM (SGR 2) ghost text:

        \\x1b[39m❯\\xa0\\x1b[2mrun the nightly rebuild

    A plain (`-p -J`) capture strips the one attribute that separates that
    ghost from text a human actually typed, so the "input line must be empty"
    gate read it as pending input and never opened again — the daemon went
    silent for hours at a stretch (2026-09-01: five consecutive theme reports
    with zero inject attempts). Colour is not decoration here; it is the only
    in-band signal for "this is a placeholder".
    """
    out, rc = run(["tmux", "capture-pane", "-t", pane, "-p", "-J", "-e"])
    if rc != 0 or not out:
        return None
    return out


# SGR sequences only — `capture-pane -e` emits nothing else.
_SGR_RE = re.compile(r"\x1b\[[0-9;]*m")


def strip_sgr(text):
    """Drop SGR escapes, leaving the glyphs a reader sees."""
    return _SGR_RE.sub("", text)


def _sgr_dim_after(params, dim):
    """Apply one SGR parameter list to the current dim state.

    Extended-colour parameters are SKIPPED rather than scanned, because
    `38;5;2` (a palette colour) contains a literal "2" and a naive scan
    would read it as SGR 2 / dim — turning every ordinary coloured run into
    a "placeholder" and reopening the mangling hazard from the other side.
    """
    fields = params.split(";") if params else ["0"]
    i = 0
    while i < len(fields):
        f = fields[i] or "0"
        if f in ("38", "48", "58"):
            nxt = fields[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(fields) else ""
            i += 3 if nxt == "5" else 5 if nxt == "2" else 1
            continue
        if f == "2":
            dim = True
        elif f in ("0", "22"):
            dim = False
        i += 1
    return dim


def _typed_input(ansi_line):
    """The NON-DIM text after `❯` on one ANSI-captured line, or None if the
    line carries no prompt glyph.

    Dim runs are dropped: they are Claude Code's ghost/placeholder suggestion,
    which occupies a box the user has not typed into. Real input is rendered
    at normal intensity, so it survives.
    """
    seen = False
    out = []
    dim = False
    pos = 0
    for m in _SGR_RE.finditer(ansi_line):
        chunk = ansi_line[pos:m.start()]
        seen, kept = _consume(chunk, seen, dim)
        out.append(kept)
        pos = m.end()
        dim = _sgr_dim_after(m.group(0)[2:-1], dim)
    seen, kept = _consume(ansi_line[pos:], seen, dim)
    out.append(kept)
    return "".join(out) if seen else None


def _consume(chunk, seen, dim):
    """Fold one attribute-uniform chunk into the prompt-line scan.

    Returns (seen_prompt_glyph, text_to_keep). Everything before the `❯` is
    box chrome; everything after it counts only when it is not dim.
    """
    if not seen:
        idx = chunk.find("❯")
        if idx < 0:
            return False, ""
        seen, chunk = True, chunk[idx + 1:]
    return seen, "" if dim else chunk


def _busy(pane_text):
    """True when a turn is in flight or work is already queued.

    Three signals, because any one alone has a blind spot:
      * "esc to interrupt" — the status-bar marker (needs `-J`, see _capture).
      * an activity/spinner line — present while generating.
      * "Press up to edit queued messages" — Claude Code QUEUES a message
        typed during a turn rather than running it. An inject that lands in
        the queue has not been applied and will not be for an unbounded time,
        so verifying it within any fixed window reports a false failure.

    Matched against the SGR-stripped text: `_capture` now keeps attributes,
    and tmux happily splits a styled run across the phrases above.
    """
    plain = strip_sgr(pane_text)
    low = plain.lower()
    if "esc to interrupt" in low:
        return True
    if "press up to edit queued messages" in low:
        return True
    return bool(_BUSY_RE.search(plain))


def _prompt_line(pane_text):
    """Text a human has actually typed on the `❯` input line, or None if no
    prompt is rendered. '' means the input line is empty.

    `pane_text` must come from `_capture` (i.e. `capture-pane -e`); dim ghost
    text is discarded, so a box showing only a suggestion reads as empty.
    """
    for line in reversed(pane_text.split("\n")):
        if "❯" in line:
            typed = _typed_input(line)
            if typed is not None:
                return typed.strip()
    return None


def idle_state(pane):
    """(is_idle, reason) -- whether it is SAFE to type into the pane, and if
    not, WHICH gate is holding it shut.

    The reason is not decoration. This gate can stay closed for hours, and
    until 2026-09-01 it did so in complete silence: the daemon logged
    "theme reported: light" and then nothing at all, which is
    indistinguishable from a dead daemon. The caller logs this string on a
    throttle so a wedged gate names itself.

    The original check -- "is the `❯` glyph anywhere in the last 15 lines"
    -- was far too weak. Claude Code renders that prompt box permanently,
    including while a turn is generating, so it reported idle for a pane that
    was mid-composition. On 2026-08-19 that let three theme injects land in an
    actively-generating pane.

    Four gates now, each closing a distinct way the old one was wrong:

      1. A prompt must actually be rendered (`❯` present).
      2. No in-flight turn -- Claude Code shows "esc to interrupt" in the
         status bar for the whole duration of one.
      3. THE INPUT LINE MUST BE EMPTY OF TYPED TEXT. This is the direct guard
         against the bug: the non-cancelling inject has no `dd` line-clear, so
         anything already on that line gets our payload glued onto it. If
         someone (or something) has half-typed input sitting there, typing is
         exactly the wrong move. "Empty" means empty of TYPED text -- Claude
         Code's dim ghost suggestion sits in an empty box and is stripped by
         `_prompt_line`; counting it as input is what wedged this gate shut.
      4. The input line must still be empty a moment later, so we don't race
         a human who just started typing.

    Deliberately NOT a full-pane stability check: the pane carries a ticking
    token counter and agent elapsed-time line, so it is never byte-stable and
    such a check would report "never idle".
    """
    out = _capture(pane)
    if out is None:
        return False, f"cannot capture pane {pane}"
    if _busy(out):
        return False, "pane busy (turn in flight or message queued)"
    typed = _prompt_line(out)
    if typed is None:
        return False, "no prompt rendered (Claude Code not at an input box?)"
    if typed != "":
        return False, f"input line not empty: {typed[:60]!r}"
    time.sleep(PROMPT_RECHECK_SECS)
    out2 = _capture(pane)
    if out2 is None:
        return False, f"cannot capture pane {pane} (recheck)"
    if _busy(out2):
        return False, "pane went busy during recheck"
    typed2 = _prompt_line(out2)
    if typed2 != "":
        return False, f"input line filled during recheck: {typed2!r}"
    return True, "idle"


def is_idle(pane):
    """True only when it is SAFE to type into the pane. See `idle_state`."""
    return idle_state(pane)[0]


def read_theme():
    """Read + validate the reported theme; return 'dark'/'light' or None."""
    try:
        val = Path(THEME_FILE).read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip().lower()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    except Exception as e:
        log(f"read error: {e}")
        return None
    if val in VALID:
        return val
    if val:
        log(f"ignoring unrecognised theme value {val!r} (want one of {VALID})")
    return None


def read_settings_theme():
    """Read the CURRENTLY APPLIED theme out of Claude Code's settings file.

    This is the ground truth for verification. `/config theme=X` hot-applies
    AND persists an explicit `theme` key here, so the file agreeing with what
    we asked for is real evidence the slash command was parsed and executed --
    unlike "the payload left the prompt line", which is equally true when
    mangled text gets submitted.

    Returns 'dark'/'light', the raw string for any other value (e.g. 'auto',
    a daltonized variant a human set by hand), or None if the file is
    missing / unreadable / not JSON.
    """
    try:
        raw = Path(SETTINGS_FILE).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    except Exception as e:
        log(f"settings read error: {e}")
        return None
    try:
        val = json.loads(raw).get("theme")
    except Exception as e:
        # A concurrent write can catch us mid-rewrite; the caller polls, so a
        # transient parse failure just costs one attempt.
        log(f"settings parse error: {e}")
        return None
    if isinstance(val, str):
        return val.strip().lower()
    return None


def verify_applied(theme):
    """Poll the settings file until it reports `theme`, up to VERIFY_SECS.

    Returns (ok, detail):
      (True,  'settings.json theme=<theme>')  -- confirmed applied.
      (False, 'settings.json theme=<other>')  -- REAL failure; /config did
                                                 not take (this is the
                                                 2026-08-19 mangled-inject
                                                 case).
      (True,  'UNVERIFIABLE: ...')            -- settings file unreadable.
                                                 We cannot prove success OR
                                                 failure, so we do NOT retry
                                                 (retrying types into the
                                                 operator's prompt line every
                                                 poll, forever) but we also
                                                 never claim "verified".
    """
    deadline = time.monotonic() + VERIFY_SECS
    seen = None
    while True:
        seen = read_settings_theme()
        if seen == theme:
            return True, f"settings.json theme={theme}"
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            break
        time.sleep(0.5)
    if seen is None:
        return True, f"UNVERIFIABLE: cannot read theme from {SETTINGS_FILE}"
    return False, f"settings.json theme={seen!r}, wanted {theme!r}"


def inject_theme(pane, theme):
    """Inject `/config theme=<theme>` as a slash command. Returns True on
    a verified submit.

    Serialised with self-clear via the shared non-blocking flock: if
    self-clear (or a prior inject) holds it, skip and retry next cycle.
    """
    try:
        lock_fd = os.open(LOCK_FILE, os.O_CREAT | os.O_RDWR, 0o644)
    except OSError as e:
        log(f"lock open failed ({e}); injecting without send-keys lock")
        lock_fd = None
    if lock_fd is not None:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(lock_fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except (OSError, BlockingIOError):
            log("send-keys lock contended (self-clear active?); retry next cycle")
            os.close(lock_fd)
            return False
    try:
        cmd = [
            "claude-watch", "inject",
            "--pane", pane,
            "--slash-command",
            # NON-CANCELLING, and deliberately NO `--escape`: inject never
            # leads with an Escape unless asked to (2026-08-18), so a theme
            # toggle can never cancel the model's in-flight turn. Types +
            # submits the slash command as a queued message (bare Enter from
            # INSERT). This is the hard guarantee behind the idle-gate above:
            # even if the pane became busy in the race window between the idle
            # check and this inject, the theme change is queued behind the
            # active turn, not interrupted. Do not add `--escape` here.
            "--submit", f"/config theme={theme}",
        ]
        out, rc = run(cmd, timeout=30)
        if rc == 0:
            # rc==0 only means the payload LEFT THE PROMPT LINE. It does not
            # mean Claude Code parsed a slash command -- a mangled payload
            # submits and clears just the same. Confirm the EFFECT instead.
            ok, detail = verify_applied(theme)
            if ok:
                log(f"injected /config theme={theme} ({detail})")
                return True
            log(
                f"inject /config theme={theme}: submit landed but theme did NOT "
                f"change ({detail}) -- payload likely mangled on the prompt line; retry"
            )
            return False
        if rc == 3:
            log(f"inject /config theme={theme}: submit NOT verified (rc=3); retry")
        else:
            log(f"inject /config theme={theme} failed rc={rc} out={out!r}; retry")
        return False
    finally:
        if lock_fd is not None:
            try:
                os.close(lock_fd)
            except Exception:
                pass


def print_status():
    pane = resolve_pane()
    want = read_theme()
    have = read_settings_theme()
    print(f"theme_file    : {THEME_FILE}")
    print(f"theme_value   : {want!r}")
    print(f"settings_file : {SETTINGS_FILE}")
    print(f"settings_theme: {have!r}")
    if want is None or have is None:
        sync = "unknown"
    elif want == have:
        sync = "in-sync"
    else:
        sync = "OUT OF SYNC (run --force to re-assert)"
    print(f"in_sync       : {sync}")
    print(f"pane          : {pane}")
    idle, reason = idle_state(pane)
    print(f"pane_idle     : {idle}")
    print(f"idle_reason   : {reason}")
    print(f"lock_file     : {LOCK_FILE}")
    print(f"log_file      : {LOG_FILE}")
    # A hook that lost its +x, or a typo'd dir, is otherwise invisible until
    # someone notices the thing it was supposed to do never happened.
    print(f"hooks_dir     : {HOOKS_DIR}")
    hooks = discover_hooks()
    print(f"hooks         : {', '.join(h.name for h in hooks) if hooks else '(none)'}")
    return 0


def force_once():
    """Re-assert the CURRENT theme-file value, ignoring what we think is
    already applied.

    Exists because the daemon is deliberately idempotent: it skips a re-inject
    when the file value equals the last value it applied, and a same-value
    write to the theme file is a no-op. That is correct for the normal path
    (the frontend re-POSTs on every page load) but it means a TUI that drifted
    out of sync -- exactly what the 2026-08-19 mangled inject left behind --
    could not be corrected without toggling the browser theme twice.

    Waits for the pane to go idle, bounded, rather than injecting into a busy
    pane. Still NON-cancelling: no --escape anywhere.
    """
    theme = read_theme()
    if theme is None:
        log(f"--force: no usable theme value in {THEME_FILE}; nothing to do")
        return 1
    pane = resolve_pane()
    deadline = time.monotonic() + FORCE_IDLE_WAIT_SECS
    while True:
        idle, reason = idle_state(pane)
        if idle:
            break
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            log(
                f"--force: pane {pane} not idle after {FORCE_IDLE_WAIT_SECS}s "
                f"({reason}); NOT injecting (would have to seize the turn to "
                f"do so)"
            )
            return 2
        time.sleep(IDLE_POLL_SECS)
    log(f"--force: re-asserting theme={theme} on pane {pane}")
    if not inject_theme(pane, theme):
        return 3
    # `applied` fires (the inject was verified); `changed` does not -- nothing
    # changed, that is the whole point of --force. OLD is empty because a
    # one-shot process has no memory of a previous value.
    run_hooks("applied", theme, old=None, reason="force", pane=pane)
    return 0


def main():
    global _reassert_requested
    argv = sys.argv[1:]
    if "--status" in argv:
        return print_status()
    force = "--force" in argv

    try:
        Path(LOG_FILE).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    except OSError:
        pass

    if _env("CLAUDE_CONTAINER_THEME_SYNC_DISABLED", "false").lower() in (
        "1", "true", "yes",
    ):
        log("CLAUDE_CONTAINER_THEME_SYNC_DISABLED set; exiting 0 (disabled)")
        return 0

    if force:
        return force_once()

    # SIGHUP => re-assert on the next poll. Registered only for the daemon
    # path; the one-shot paths above have already returned.
    try:
        signal.signal(signal.SIGHUP, _on_sighup)
    except (ValueError, OSError):
        pass

    log(
        f"START: watching {THEME_FILE} (poll {POLL_SECS}s), "
        f"verify={SETTINGS_FILE}, legacy lock={LOCK_FILE}"
    )

    applied = None          # last theme we VERIFIED as applied
    desired = None          # last theme read from the file (may await idle)
    desired_reason = "startup"  # CW_THEME_REASON for `desired`'s hook fires
    last_mtime = None
    verify_failures = 0     # consecutive failed verifications of `desired`
    retry_not_before = 0.0  # monotonic deadline enforcing RETRY_BACKOFF_SECS
    pending_since = None    # when `desired` first went un-appliable
    blocked_logged_at = 0.0 # throttle for the "waiting for idle" line

    while True:
        # SIGHUP asks us to re-assert even though the file value has not
        # changed.
        if _reassert_requested:
            _reassert_requested = False
            log("SIGHUP: re-asserting current theme (forgetting applied state)")
            applied = None
            # No `changed` fire: the file value has not moved. The re-assert
            # that follows carries REASON=sighup into its `applied` fire.
            desired_reason = "sighup"
            verify_failures = 0
            retry_not_before = 0.0
            last_mtime = None

        try:
            mtime = os.path.getmtime(THEME_FILE)
        except OSError:
            mtime = None
        if mtime != last_mtime:
            last_mtime = mtime
            val = read_theme()
            if val and val != desired:
                log(f"theme reported: {val}")
                previous = desired
                desired = val
                desired_reason = "startup" if previous is None else "change"
                verify_failures = 0
                retry_not_before = 0.0
                # HOOKS FIRE HERE -- on the validated value flip, BEFORE the
                # idle gate below. Deliberately not after it: the gate can
                # stay shut for hours, and an external side effect must not
                # inherit that unbounded latency. Anything that genuinely
                # needs the TUI to have caught up uses `applied` instead.
                run_hooks("changed", val, old=previous, reason=desired_reason)

        if desired and desired != applied:
            # Backoff gate: after a failed attempt we wait out
            # RETRY_BACKOFF_SECS before touching the pane again.
            if time.monotonic() < retry_not_before:
                time.sleep(min(IDLE_POLL_SECS, POLL_SECS))
                continue

            pane = resolve_pane()
            idle, idle_reason = idle_state(pane)
            if idle:
                pending_since = None
                blocked_logged_at = 0.0
                if inject_theme(pane, desired):
                    previously_applied = applied
                    applied = desired
                    verify_failures = 0
                    retry_not_before = 0.0
                    # Only reached on a VERIFIED inject -- never on a failed
                    # one, and never on the GIVING UP path below.
                    run_hooks(
                        "applied", applied, old=previously_applied,
                        reason=desired_reason, pane=pane,
                    )
                else:
                    verify_failures += 1
                    if verify_failures >= MAX_VERIFY_FAILURES:
                        # Stop hammering the operator's prompt line. Mark it
                        # applied so we go quiet until the theme file changes
                        # again (or someone sends SIGHUP / runs --force), and
                        # say so LOUDLY -- this is the state the old code hid
                        # behind a bogus "(verified)".
                        log(
                            f"GIVING UP on theme={desired} after "
                            f"{verify_failures} failed verifications; the TUI is "
                            f"OUT OF SYNC with {THEME_FILE}. Investigate, then "
                            f"re-assert with `cw-theme-sync --force` or SIGHUP."
                        )
                        applied = desired
                        verify_failures = 0
                        retry_not_before = 0.0
                    else:
                        backoff = RETRY_BACKOFF_SECS[
                            min(verify_failures - 1, len(RETRY_BACKOFF_SECS) - 1)
                        ]
                        retry_not_before = time.monotonic() + backoff
                        log(
                            f"backing off {backoff:.0f}s before retry "
                            f"{verify_failures + 1}/{MAX_VERIFY_FAILURES} of "
                            f"theme={desired}"
                        )
                time.sleep(POLL_SECS)
                continue

            # Gate shut. Say so, on a throttle: a theme that never lands used
            # to look exactly like a dead daemon, because waiting produced no
            # output at all. One line per BLOCKED_LOG_SECS names the gate and
            # how long the change has been stuck behind it.
            now = time.monotonic()
            if pending_since is None:
                pending_since = now
            if now - blocked_logged_at >= BLOCKED_LOG_SECS:
                blocked_logged_at = now
                log(
                    f"theme={desired} pending {now - pending_since:.0f}s: "
                    f"waiting for an idle pane -- {idle_reason}"
                )
            time.sleep(min(IDLE_POLL_SECS, POLL_SECS))
            continue

        pending_since = None
        blocked_logged_at = 0.0
        time.sleep(POLL_SECS)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(0)
